Defects found:

- SELECTOR KEYED TO A FORM THE CORPUS DOES NOT USE: line 53.  
  Exact input: `empty_reviews_notice.json={"cleanup_zeroed":[{"review":"R"}],"never_extracted":[]}` and `index.html='<a class="card x" href="R.html">R</a>\n'`.  
  Consequence: the review HTML can be deleted while the index card remains as a broken link.

- SELECTOR KEYED TO A FORM THE CORPUS DOES NOT USE: line 86.  
  Exact input: same notice, with `index.html='<tr><td>Area</td><td><a href="R.html">R</a></td></tr>'`.  
  Consequence: a review table row containing the removed review is left behind because the link is not in the first cell.

- INSTRUMENT READING A DIFFERENT COPY THAN THE ONE BEING CHANGED: line 97.  
  Exact input: same notice, `index.html` contains `<a href="R.html" class="card x">R</a>`, but `R.html` is already absent on disk.  
  Consequence: the card is removed from `index.html`, but `remaining_count` stays `411`, so the displayed count is wrong.

- SELECTOR KEYED TOO BROADLY: lines 99-101.  
  Exact input: same notice with existing `R.html`, and `index.html='<p>PMID 421</p><h1>411 reviews</h1>'`.  
  Consequence: unrelated numeric content such as `PMID 421` is rewritten to `410`.

- STATUS/REPORTING THE WRAPPER RATHER THAN THE WORK: line 114.  
  Exact input: run `python scripts/remove_empty_reviews.py --dry-run` with any nonempty notice file.  
  Consequence: dry-run still writes `outputs/extraction_audit/removed_empty_reviews.json`, recording hypothetical removals as a real workspace mutation.

No proven defect in this packet for: probe keyed to string a fix removes; idempotency guard satisfied by own comment; CRLF/LF text comparison; merged-cell/off-by-one tabulation; zero conflated with absent; negation dropped.
